Below is a **complete, ready-to-copy** example that shows how to:

1. **Add a button** to the page (or any UI element you already have).  
2. **Listen for the click** and call `map.flyTo()` (or `map.easeTo()` if you want a slightly different easing curve).  
3. Keep the animation **smooth and predictable** by setting explicit `duration`, `speed`, `curve`, and optional `essential` flag (helps with reduced-motion accessibility settings).  
4. Preserve the **layer order / label visibility** – the camera move does not touch the style, so nothing else needs to be changed.

---

## 1. Minimal HTML

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre – Fly to location on button click</title>
  <meta name="viewport" content="initial-scale=1,maximum-scale=1,user-scalable=no" />
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    body { margin:0; padding:0; }
    #map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%; }
    .fly-btn {
      position: absolute;
      top: 10px; left: 10px;
      padding: 8px 12px;
      background: rgba(255,255,255,0.9);
      border: 1px solid #777;
      border-radius: 4px;
      cursor: pointer;
      font-family: sans-serif;
    }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>
<button id="flyBtn" class="fly-btn">Fly to Paris</button>

<script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
<script src="fly-to.js"></script>
</body>
</html>
```

*You can put the JavaScript inline or in a separate file (`fly-to.js`).*

---

## 2. JavaScript – Initialise the map and add the fly-to logic

```js
// fly-to.js ---------------------------------------------------------

// 1️⃣ Initialise the map (replace the source URLs with your own if needed)
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',                     // HTML element id
  style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json', // any vector style
  center: [-0.1276, 51.5074],           // London (lon, lat)
  zoom: 10,
  pitch: 0,
  bearing: 0
});

// Optional: add navigation controls (zoom, compass) for testing
map.addControl(new maplibregl.NavigationControl());

// 2️⃣ Define the target camera parameters
const parisCamera = {
  // lon/lat of the centre you want to fly to
  center: [2.3522, 48.8566],   // Paris
  // Desired zoom – pick something that shows the city nicely
  zoom: 12,
  // Optional: tilt the map a little for a 3-D feel
  pitch: 45,
  // Optional: rotate so north points slightly off-screen (just for demo)
  bearing: -30,
  // Animation options -------------------------------------------------
  //   - duration (ms) – total time of the animation
  //   - speed (units per second) – overrides duration if set
  //   - curve (0-1) – easing curve; 1.42 is the default “fly” curve
  //   - essential – true tells browsers to ignore reduced-motion prefs
  //                 (set false if you want to respect them)
  duration: 2500,
  curve: 1.42,
  essential: true
};

// 3️⃣ Hook the button click
document.getElementById('flyBtn').addEventListener('click', () => {
  // Use flyTo for the classic “smooth, accelerating-then-decelerating” motion
  map.flyTo(parisCamera);

  // If you prefer a linear-easing, you could use:
  // map.easeTo({ ...parisCamera, curve: 0, duration: 1500 });
});

// 4️⃣ (Optional) Keep focus on the map for keyboard users
// When the button is activated, move focus to the map canvas so
// arrow keys continue to pan/rotate.
document.getElementById('flyBtn').addEventListener('click', () => {
  map.getCanvas().focus();
});
```

### What each option does

| Option | Why it matters for a *smooth* fly |
|--------|-----------------------------------|
| `center` | Destination lon/lat. |
| `zoom`   | Controls how “close” you get. Larger zoom → larger visual change, so the animation will feel longer unless you also adjust `duration`. |
| `pitch`  | Adds a 3-D tilt; the camera will smoothly tilt while moving. |
| `bearing`| Rotates the map; the rotation is interpolated together with the pan/zoom. |
| `duration`| Fixed time in ms. If you want the same *feel* regardless of distance, use `speed` instead. |
| `speed`   | Units per second (default 1.2). Overrides `duration` if supplied. |
| `curve`   | The easing curve. `1.42` is the classic “fly” curve (accelerates then decelerates). Smaller values → more linear; larger → more “overshoot”. |
| `essential`| Set to `true` to **ignore** the user’s “prefers-reduced-motion” setting (useful for critical UI flows). Set to `false` if you want the animation to be suppressed for those users. |

---

## 3. Why `flyTo` (vs. `easeTo`)?

* `flyTo` implements the **“optimal flight path”** algorithm described in the Mapbox GL JS docs (and inherited by MapLibre). It automatically chooses a smooth arc that keeps the camera’s speed roughly constant, even when the distance is large.  
* `easeTo` is a generic linear-easing move; it’s fine for short hops or when you want a *different* easing curve (e.g., `curve: 0` for a straight linear interpolation).

Both functions **do not affect layer ordering** – they only change the view matrix, so your basemap labels, roads, and any custom data stay exactly where they belong.

---

## 4. Accessibility tip – respect reduced-motion

If you want to **honour** the user’s “prefers-reduced-motion” media query, wrap the call:

```js
const prefersReduced = window.matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)').matches;

if (!prefersReduced) {
  map.flyTo(parisCamera);   // normal animation
} else {
  // Jump instantly (or use a very short duration)
  map.jumpTo({ center: parisCamera.center, zoom: parisCamera.zoom, pitch: parisCamera.pitch, bearing: parisCamera.bearing });
}
```

Or simply set `essential: false` in the options object; MapLibre will automatically suppress the animation for users who have reduced-motion enabled.

---

## 5. Full-page example (copy-paste)

If you just want a single file to test, paste the following into a new `index.html` and open it in a browser:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre – Fly to Paris</title>
  <meta name="viewport" content="initial-scale=1,maximum-scale=1,user-scalable=no" />
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    body,html {margin:0;padding:0;height:100%;}
    #map {position:absolute;top:0;bottom:0;width:100%;}
    .fly-btn {
      position:absolute;top:10px;left:10px;
      padding:8px 12px;background:rgba(255,255,255,0.9);
      border:1px solid #777;border-radius:4px;cursor:pointer;
      font-family:sans-serif;
    }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>
<button id="flyBtn" class="fly-btn">Fly to Paris</button>

<script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
<script>
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json',
    center: [-0.1276, 51.5074],
    zoom: 10
  });
  map.addControl(new maplibregl.NavigationControl());

  const parisCamera = {
    center: [2.3522, 48.8566],
    zoom: 12,
    pitch: 45,
    bearing: -30,
    duration: 2500,
    curve: 1.42,
    essential: true
  };

  document.getElementById('flyBtn').addEventListener('click', () => {
    const reduced = window.matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)').matches;
    if (reduced) {
      map.jumpTo(parisCamera);   // instant move
    } else {
      map.flyTo(parisCamera);
    }
    map.getCanvas().focus();      // keep keyboard focus on the map
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

Open the file, click **“Fly to Paris”**, and watch the smooth animation.

---

### TL;DR Checklist

- ✅ Use `map.flyTo({center, zoom, pitch, bearing, duration, curve, essential})`.  
- ✅ Set `duration` (ms) or `speed` (units / sec) to control timing.  
- ✅ Add `curve` (default 1.42) for the classic “fly” easing.  
- ✅ Respect `prefers-reduced-motion` (set `essential: false` or fallback to `jumpTo`).  
- ✅ Keep UI focus on the map after the click for keyboard users.  
- ✅ No layer-order changes needed – the camera move is independent of style layers.

That’s all you need to give users a polished, smooth-fly experience in MapLibre GL JS! 🚀
